{{FIG:model_benchmark_baseline|Baseline benchmark of the five candidate models at $t=100\%$ on the held-out test set: recall, F1, PR-AUC, and ROC-AUC per model (no resampling, shared seed 42).}}

XGBoost tops the recall-first ranking with an at-risk recall of {{VAL:XGB_T100_RECALL}} and a PR-AUC of {{VAL:XGB_T100_PRAUC}} ({{VAL:CI_PRAUC_FULL_T100}}). Two structural observations matter more than the winner's identity. First, the three tree ensembles track each other closely on all seven metrics---the boosting family, not one implementation, dominates. Second, logistic regression trails by only a few points, indicating that the engineered features carry a strong, largely linearly separable signal on which the ensembles add a real but modest increment.

\subsection{Is the Ranking Real?}

Small between-model gaps demand verification. Table~\ref{tab:cv_summary} summarises the repeated cross-validation (5 folds $\times$ 5 seeds, 25 fits per model, preprocessing re-fitted per fold) as mean $\pm$ standard deviation.

\begin{table*}[t]
\centering
\caption{Repeated cross-validation on the training partition (5-fold $\times$ 5 seeds; 25 fits per model, $t=100\%$): mean $\pm$ standard deviation per model and metric.}
\label{tab:cv_summary}
\footnotesize
{{TEX:cv_summary}}
\end{table*}

{{FIG:cv_uncertainty|Cross-validated recall, F1, and PR-AUC per model as mean $\pm$ one standard deviation over the 25 fits (5-fold $\times$ 5 seeds, $t=100\%$): between-model gaps exceed within-model variability.}}

XGBoost leads cross-validated recall at {{VAL:CV_XGB_RECALL}}, with LightGBM immediately behind, and the CV ranking reproduces the held-out ranking---the single-split benchmark is not an outlier. The Friedman test over the 25 paired fits rejects equal performance far below the 0.05 level on every metric, so the small observed differences are systematic rather than noise; Holm-corrected pairwise Wilcoxon signed-rank tests sharpen the picture, showing XGBoost significantly ahead of each of the other four models on recall while LightGBM leads the composite metrics (F1, PR-AUC, ROC-AUC). Given the recall-first objective and the exact TreeExplainer attributions required by the explanation analysis, XGBoost is selected as the primary model.

In bias--variance terms the two verification layers also explain why the ranking is trustworthy. The per-model standard deviations are small relative to between-model gaps---the estimators are low-variance at this sample size---and overfitting is bounded by construction: every score is computed on data the model never saw (25 held-out folds plus the frozen test set), preprocessing is re-fitted inside each fold, and the close agreement between cross-validated and held-out rankings confirms that no model is merely memorising its training fold. Underfitting is visible instead as the systematic few-point gap between the linear baseline and the tree ensembles: logistic regression's higher-bias hypothesis class cannot represent the feature interactions the boosted trees exploit, and that difference is precisely the increment the ensembles add.

\subsection{Time-Aware Performance on the Full Cohort}

The identical protocol was repeated at all six checkpoints on the same frozen test students.

{{FIG:time_aware_recall|At-risk recall of the best model per checkpoint across the six course-progress checkpoints on the full test cohort; the horizontal line marks the recall $\geq 0.80$ reliability criterion.}}

LightGBM is narrowly ahead at the two earliest checkpoints (both below the reliability bar) and XGBoost from $t=40\%$ onward. On the full enrolment cohort, the recall point estimate crosses 0.80 at $t=40\%$, reaching {{VAL:XGB_T40_RECALL}}, and rises monotonically to {{VAL:XGB_T100_RECALL}} at course end, with PR-AUC comfortably above its criterion over the same range---so recall, not ranking quality, is the binding constraint on earliness. The trajectory is consistent with the 40--60\% reliability window of \cite{adnan2021} on the same dataset. The bootstrap, however, imposes a nuance invisible in point-estimate reporting: the interval at $t=40\%$ is {{VAL:CI_RECALL_FULL_T40}}, whose lower bound touches the criterion. The honest full-cohort statement is therefore that reliability at 40\% of course length is \emph{plausible but borderline} under sampling uncertainty, firming up only at later checkpoints (at course end, {{VAL:CI_RECALL_FULL_T100}}).

\subsection{Whom Are We Really Predicting? The Dual-Cohort Result}

The full cohort is only one of the two estimands of Section~III-B. Because the at-risk label includes withdrawal and the roster is fixed, the test set at every checkpoint contains students who have already left---{{VAL:GONE_T10_TEST}} test enrolments before the earliest checkpoint at $t=10\%$, and more at each later cutoff. For these records the model records an outcome that has already happened. Table~\ref{tab:dual_cohort} therefore re-scores the \emph{same} XGBoost predictions on the still-enrolled subpopulation at each checkpoint, with bootstrap intervals for both cohorts and for their gap.

