\centering
\caption{Dual-cohort re-scoring of the selected XGBoost model per checkpoint: at-risk recall on the full test cohort and on the still-enrolled subpopulation, with 95\% percentile-bootstrap confidence intervals (1,000 student-level resamples of the frozen test set) and the between-cohort recall gap.}
\label{tab:dual_cohort}
\footnotesize
{{TEX:dual_cohort_ci}}
\end{table*}

{{FIG:sensitivity_active_recall_xgb|XGBoost at-risk recall per checkpoint on the full test cohort versus the still-enrolled subpopulation, with the recall $\geq 0.80$ criterion marked; the gap between the curves quantifies the contribution of already-withdrawn students to measured performance.}}

The gap is substantial, systematic, and statistically unambiguous. On the still-enrolled cohort, recall is {{VAL:ACTIVE_T40_RECALL}} at $t=40\%$ ({{VAL:CI_RECALL_ACTIVE_T40}}) and {{VAL:ACTIVE_T80_RECALL}} at $t=80\%$, and reaches the criterion only at course end: {{VAL:ACTIVE_T100_RECALL}} ({{VAL:CI_RECALL_ACTIVE_T100}}). The bootstrap interval for the between-cohort gap excludes zero at every checkpoint---{{VAL:CI_GAP_T40}} at $t=40\%$ and still {{VAL:CI_GAP_T100}} at $t=100\%$, where the residual gap reflects that at-risk students still enrolled at course end (imminent failers rather than withdrawers) are intrinsically harder to detect. LightGBM reproduces the same qualitative shape, so the finding is not specific to one model. Note that nothing about the model changes between the two rows of any checkpoint pair: the predictions are identical and only the evaluation population changes. The difference between the curves is a pure population effect---exactly the quantity a reader needs in order to translate a published full-cohort number into an expectation about intervention yield.

The dual-cohort answer to the earliness question is therefore stated in two parts, always together. On the full enrolment cohort---the frame in which the base studies \cite{tomasevic2020,adnan2021} operate---XGBoost is the best model from $t=40\%$ onward and reliability is plausible, though borderline under the bootstrap, from that checkpoint. On the still-enrolled cohort---the frame in which a tutor can still act---the same criterion is met only at course end, and mid-course predictions, while informative, fall short of the bar.

Two clarifications guard against misreading. First, this is \emph{not} data leakage: the label never enters the features, and the near-total inactivity of an early-withdrawn student is genuinely observed behaviour. Second, it is a \emph{population-definition} result: part of full-cohort performance at every checkpoint comes from recognising students who have already gone---legitimate for outcome classification, but not early warning. The answer to which checkpoint yields ``reliable'' prediction thus changes by three checkpoints depending on a population choice that prior work does not report, which is precisely why we propose that both frames always be published together.

\subsection{Operating Thresholds and Subgroup Fairness}

A deployed system needs a decision threshold, and choosing it on the test set would optimistically bias every reported metric. Thresholds are therefore selected on out-of-fold validation predictions from cross-validation on the training partition, frozen, and scored on the test set exactly once per policy. The F1-optimal policy selects {{VAL:THR_F1}}, nearly coinciding with the default 0.5, and its validation metrics transfer to the test set with negligible drift---retrospective evidence that the default-threshold results above were not tuned on the test set. An institutional high-recall policy is also quantified: mandating recall $\geq 0.90$ yields a validation-chosen threshold of {{VAL:THR_R90}} that delivers the mandated recall on the test set at a precision of {{VAL:THR_R90_TEST_PRECISION}}---an operating point at which flagged students are almost always genuinely at risk. The full validation trade-off curves are committed alongside the result tables, so an institution can select any other operating point without ever touching the test set.

For the selected model at $t=100\%$ and the default threshold, recall and false-positive rate were disaggregated over every level of six demographic attributes (gender, region, deprivation band, prior education, age band, declared disability; subgroups with at least 50 test members). Table~\ref{tab:fairness} summarises the within-attribute ranges: the largest recall gap across all attributes is {{VAL:FAIR_MAX_GAP_PP}} percentage points, observed on {{VAL:FAIR_MAX_GAP_ATTR}}, with false-positive-rate gaps of similarly modest order and no subgroup---including students declaring a disability---falling dramatically below the overall recall level. These are observed disparities at one checkpoint and threshold, not a fairness certification; a deployment should monitor the same disaggregated metrics continuously.

