\subsection{Robustness to Imbalance Handling}

Because the at-risk class is a slight majority (52.8\%, ratio 1.12), resampling here answers a robustness question, not a rare-class rescue---indeed, default-configured SMOTE \cite{chawla2002} and ADASYN oversample the \emph{not-at-risk} class on this dataset, the opposite of the textbook ``rescue the rare at-risk class'' intuition, a behaviour we detected early and document rather than let pass unremarked. Every resampling or weighting intervention is applied to the training partition only, after preprocessing has been fitted; the held-out test set is never resampled and always reflects the real class distribution. Retraining all five models at $t=100\%$ under four regimes (none, class weighting, SMOTE, ADASYN) moves no primary metric materially: the largest spread on at-risk recall, F1, or PR-AUC across all models and strategies is {{VAL:IMB_MAX_SPREAD}}, of the same order as seed-to-seed cross-validation variation, with no strategy consistently ahead. This is the expected behaviour at an imbalance ratio of 1.12: with both classes abundantly represented, neither synthetic oversampling nor loss reweighting has room to move the decision boundary. Predictive conclusions on OULAD under this label mapping therefore do not depend on the imbalance-handling choice; Section~V-C shows the same holds for explanations.

\section{Explanation Stability}

\subsection{What Drives an At-Risk Flag}

Global SHAP values for the selected model are computed with TreeExplainer, which is exact for tree ensembles \cite{lundberg2017}.

{{FIG:shap_importance_xgb_t100|Global SHAP feature importance (mean absolute SHAP value) for XGBoost at $t=100\%$. The length of the student's current VLE silence dominates, followed by the cumulative weighted assessment score.}}

One feature dominates by a wide margin: \texttt{days\_since\_last\_activity}, the length of the student's current silence in the VLE, followed by \texttt{weighted\_score\_to\_date}, the cumulative weighted assessment score. Attribution directions match tutor intuition---long silence pushes strongly toward a flag, a high running score pulls away---and the remaining contributors (submission counts, module identity, per-activity click features) act in similarly plausible directions, so the model's strongest signals are precisely the two indicators human tutors already watch: disengagement and weak assessment performance. The dominance of a silence feature is also an inside-the-model corroboration of Section~IV-D: on the full cohort---where recall is {{VAL:XGB_T100_RECALL}} versus {{VAL:ACTIVE_T100_RECALL}} on the still-enrolled subgroup---\texttt{days\_since\_last\_activity} is exactly the channel through which already-disengaged and withdrawn students are recognised rather than forecast. The explanation layer thus confirms, mechanistically, why every headline claim must name its cohort. Because global importances do not help a tutor with an individual student, LIME \cite{ribeiro2016} supplies the local ``why''---the form in which an explanation is actually consumed in an intervention conversation, and the form served per student by the dashboard.

\subsection{Stability Against the Null Baseline}

An explanation can support intervention decisions only if it is reproducible, and an agreement score can support a stability claim only if it is compared against chance. All agreement statistics below are therefore read against the Monte-Carlo null of Section~III-F: two random top-10 draws from the 49-feature space overlap with mean Jaccard {{VAL:JB_MEAN}}, 95th percentile {{VAL:JB_P95}}, and 99th percentile {{VAL:JB_P99}}. Without this calibration, the partial SHAP--LIME overlap reported below could be spun as either reassuring or alarming; against the null, its meaning is unambiguous.

\textbf{Across seeds.} Retraining the selected model under five seeds and re-explaining yields a mean top-10 Jaccard of {{VAL:SEED_JACCARD}} and a mean Spearman rank correlation of {{VAL:SEED_SPEARMAN}} across seed pairs---far above the null's 99th percentile. The global feature story is a property of the data and model class, not an artefact of one training run.

\textbf{Across explainers.} SHAP and LIME agree partially: the Jaccard overlap of their global top-10 sets is {{VAL:SHAPLIME_JACCARD}}, computed on a LIME sample of $n=100$ test students. This too exceeds the null's 99th percentile---the agreement is genuine---but it is far from unity, and the mechanism is methodological rather than pathological: SHAP allocates exact Shapley contributions of the trained model, whereas LIME fits a local linear surrogate to perturbed inputs and perturbs one-hot categories as if continuous, injecting noise into mid-ranking features. The two explainers agree on the headline drivers and diverge in the tail. Our policy: anchor global claims on SHAP (exact for trees), use LIME as a per-student local cross-check, and report the disagreement rather than selecting the more flattering explainer.

\subsection{Stability Across Training Regimes}

Retraining XGBoost at $t=100\%$ once per imbalance-handling regime and comparing global SHAP rankings pairwise (Table~\ref{tab:xai_strategies}) shows Spearman correlations close to unity for all six strategy pairs, with moderate top-10 Jaccard variation: the dominant features are invariant to the training regime while the tail of the top-10 shuffles mildly. A tutor would be told substantially the same story under any of the four regimes---the explanation-side counterpart of the accuracy-side robustness in Section~IV-F.

